1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:11,584
This is STAT 331, video 2 a. It covers Lecture 1, slides 21 to 25: the idea of regression, and the normal distribution.

2
00:00:12,034 --> 00:00:18,498
This video prepares you for four question types. Each type gets a method card and worked problems.

3
00:00:18,948 --> 00:00:24,281
Type A: find the change in the mean of y when x increases by an amount a.

4
00:00:24,731 --> 00:00:29,745
Type B: read a regression model and its data table, symbol by symbol.

5
00:00:30,195 --> 00:00:33,587
Type C: evaluate the normal density at a point.

6
00:00:34,037 --> 00:00:45,343
Type D: find the distribution of a linear combination of independent normal variables. Lectures 2 and 3 use it for y i and for beta 1 hat.

7
00:00:45,793 --> 00:00:58,508
Here is the Gladstone data from Lecture 1. Each blue point is one person: head size and brain weight. Question 1: how do we describe the relationship between x and y?

8
00:00:58,958 --> 00:01:13,593
Question 2: how do we predict y for a given x? For example, a person has a head size of 4000 cubic centimeters. Which brain weight do we expect? The purple strip shows the people near this x.

9
00:01:14,043 --> 00:01:26,160
Question 3: how does the mean of y change when x increases by an amount a? A straight line answers this. Move a to the right along the line, and read the change in height.

10
00:01:26,610 --> 00:01:37,831
Simple linear regression answers all three questions. The model is: y i equals beta 0, plus beta 1 times x i, plus epsilon i.

11
00:01:38,281 --> 00:01:52,340
Look at each symbol of the model. The index i is the row number. Row i is one person, with covariate x i and outcome y i. The dashed line is beta 0 plus beta 1 x.

12
00:01:52,790 --> 00:02:01,899
Beta 0 is the intercept. It is the height of the line at x equals 0. It is a parameter: a fixed number that we do not know.

13
00:02:02,349 --> 00:02:12,141
Beta 1 is the slope. When x increases by 1, the line height increases by beta 1. It is also a fixed, unknown parameter.

14
00:02:12,591 --> 00:02:27,482
Epsilon i is the error of row i. It is the vertical distance from the point to the line: y i minus beta 0 plus beta 1 x i. This point is above the line, so its error is positive.

15
00:02:27,932 --> 00:02:38,172
This second point is below the line, so its error is negative. Beta 0 and beta 1 are the same for every row. Each row has its own error.

16
00:02:38,622 --> 00:02:44,062
Slide 22 draws all 236 people of the Gladstone data.

17
00:02:44,512 --> 00:02:58,379
It adds one straight line through the points. Lecture 2 shows how to calculate this line. Its intercept is 335 point 4 3, and its slope is 0 point 2 6 0 8.

18
00:02:58,829 --> 00:03:19,629
Row 1 has head size 4512 and brain weight 1530. The line height at 4512 is 1512 point 3. The point is above the line. Its vertical distance is plus 17 point 7 grams.

19
00:03:20,079 --> 00:03:36,996
Row 4 has head size 3777 and brain weight 1282. The line height is 1320 point 6. The point is below the line, by 38 point 6 grams.

20
00:03:37,446 --> 00:03:50,118
In total, 116 points are above the line, and 120 are below. No line goes through all the points. So the model needs an error term for each observation.

21
00:03:50,568 --> 00:04:02,472
Question type A asks: how does the mean of y change when x increases by an amount a? This is question 3 of slide 21. Method card A has four steps.

22
00:04:02,922 --> 00:04:10,666
Step 1: write the mean of y at x. It is beta 0 plus beta 1 x, the height of the line at x.

23
00:04:11,116 --> 00:04:17,900
Step 2: write the mean at x plus a. It is beta 0 plus beta 1 times x plus a.

24
00:04:18,350 --> 00:04:26,222
Step 3: subtract the first mean from the second. The change is beta 1 times a. The purple segment shows it.

25
00:04:26,672 --> 00:04:37,424
Step 4: put in the estimate beta 1 hat and the value of a, and give the units of y. With an estimate, the answer is a change in the estimated mean.

26
00:04:37,874 --> 00:04:56,455
We follow card A. Pause the video and try it. Step 1: the model says y i equals beta 0 plus beta 1 x i plus epsilon i, and epsilon i has mean 0. So the mean of y at x is beta 0 plus beta 1 x.

27
00:04:56,905 --> 00:05:03,007
Step 2: at x plus a, the mean is beta 0 plus beta 1 times x plus a.

28
00:05:03,457 --> 00:05:06,422
Step 3: subtract line 1 from line 2.

29
00:05:06,872 --> 00:05:11,224
Expand the brackets. The new term is beta 1 a, in orange.

30
00:05:11,674 --> 00:05:21,679
Beta 0 cancels, and beta 1 x cancels. The change is beta 1 times a. In the picture, it is the height of the purple segment.

31
00:05:22,129 --> 00:05:31,089
With a equals 1, the change is beta 1. This is the meaning of the slope. Question type A uses this result in step 3.

32
00:05:31,539 --> 00:05:56,585
A type A question. Pause the video and try it. In the Gladstone data, x is head size and y is brain weight. Lecture 2 gives beta 0 hat 335 point 4 3 2 3, and beta 1 hat 0 point 2 6 0 8. Head size increases by 100 cubic centimeters. Find the change in the estimated mean brain weight.

33
00:05:57,035 --> 00:06:15,573
Step 1 of card A: the estimated mean at x is 335 point 4 3 2 3 plus 0 point 2 6 0 8 x. The picture zooms into the data near x equals 3600. The green dot is the mean at x.

34
00:06:16,023 --> 00:06:29,271
Step 2: the estimated mean at x plus 100 is 335 point 4 3 2 3 plus 0 point 2 6 0 8 times x plus 100.

35
00:06:29,721 --> 00:06:38,084
Step 3: subtract. The change is beta 1 hat times a: 0 point 2 6 0 8 times 100.

36
00:06:38,534 --> 00:06:50,417
Step 4: that is 26 point 0 8. The units of y are grams. So the estimated mean brain weight increases by 26 point 0 8 grams.

37
00:06:50,867 --> 00:07:14,547
A second type A question, from Tutorial 1. Pause the video and try it. x is machine hours per month, and y is the monthly cost in hundreds of dollars. The fitted line is y hat equals 68 point 4 5 minus 0 point 4 1 0 4 x. Find the change in the estimated mean cost for 10 more hours.

38
00:07:14,997 --> 00:07:21,738
Steps 1 and 2 of card A: write the estimated mean at x plus 10 and at x, and subtract.

39
00:07:22,188 --> 00:07:33,089
Step 3: the change is beta 1 hat times a: minus 0 point 4 1 0 4 times 10. The purple segment shows the 10 hours.

40
00:07:33,539 --> 00:07:40,409
Step 4: that is minus 4 point 1 0 4. The units of y are hundreds of dollars.

41
00:07:40,859 --> 00:07:55,472
Minus 4 point 1 0 4 times 100 dollars is minus 410 dollars and 40 cents. So the estimated mean monthly cost decreases by 410 dollars and 40 cents.

42
00:07:55,922 --> 00:08:07,655
Slide 23 prints the first rows of the data file. Each row is one person. The file has four columns: gender, age group, head size, and brain weight.

43
00:08:08,105 --> 00:08:13,887
Brain weight is the outcome, y. It is the variable whose mean we want to explain.

44
00:08:14,337 --> 00:08:20,566
The other three columns are possible covariates. Until now, we used only head size.

45
00:08:21,016 --> 00:08:37,187
Gender and age contain text labels: gender is M or F, and age is the group 20 to 46, or 46 and older. Head size and brain weight contain numbers. Later in the course, text columns become numbers.

46
00:08:37,637 --> 00:08:45,445
The data has four columns and one outcome. So there are four minus one, which is three, possible covariates.

47
00:08:45,895 --> 00:09:03,452
The slide also gives a summary of each number column. For head size, the smallest value is 2773 and the largest value is 4747 cubic centimeters. The blue line spans this range.

48
00:09:03,902 --> 00:09:23,209
The box spans the first and third quartiles, 3390 and 3876. About half of the people are in the box. The thick line in the box is the median, 3614: the middle value of the sorted column.

49
00:09:23,659 --> 00:09:31,936
The purple dot is the mean, 3638. It is near the median, so the column is close to symmetric.

50
00:09:32,386 --> 00:09:51,735
For brain weight: the range is 1012 to 1635 grams. The quartiles are 1208 and 1351. The median is 1280, and the mean is 1284.

51
00:09:52,185 --> 00:09:58,265
Slide 24 compares two models. The simple model has one covariate, x i.

52
00:09:58,715 --> 00:10:13,051
The multiple linear regression model has more covariates. Here it has three: beta 1 times x i 1, plus beta 2 times x i 2, plus beta 3 times x i 3, plus the error.

53
00:10:13,501 --> 00:10:26,856
The covariate now has two indices. x i j is covariate j of row i. The first index is the row. The second index is the column. Covariate 1 is head size.

54
00:10:27,306 --> 00:10:37,653
For example, x 5 2 is covariate 2 of row 5. Covariate 2 is gender, so x 5 2 is the gender of person 5: M.

55
00:10:38,103 --> 00:10:52,353
Count the parameters. There is one beta for each covariate, plus the intercept beta 0. Three covariates give four parameters. No beta has the index i: the betas are the same for all rows.

56
00:10:52,803 --> 00:11:05,113
Keep only covariate 1 and write x i 1 as x i. Then you get the simple model again. So simple linear regression is a special case of multiple linear regression.

57
00:11:05,563 --> 00:11:13,477
Slide 24 also gives the plan of the course. The course develops multiple linear regression in four ways.

58
00:11:13,927 --> 00:11:24,317
First, theory. We derive estimators of the betas mathematically. An estimator is a formula that calculates an unknown parameter from data.

59
00:11:24,767 --> 00:11:28,628
Second, practice. We fit the models to data with software.

60
00:11:29,078 --> 00:11:35,841
Third, model choice. We choose and compare models: which covariates x i j to include.

61
00:11:36,291 --> 00:11:42,243
Fourth, diagnostics. We check if the model and its assumptions are appropriate for the data.

62
00:11:42,693 --> 00:11:53,317
Question type B asks you to read a model: name each symbol, find a covariate value, and count the parameters. Method card B has four steps.

63
00:11:53,767 --> 00:12:01,191
Step 1: find the outcome y. It is the variable whose mean the model explains. Here it is brain weight.

64
00:12:01,641 --> 00:12:09,321
Step 2: the covariates are the other columns in the model. x i j is covariate j of row i.

65
00:12:09,771 --> 00:12:19,520
Step 3: count the parameters. There is one beta for each covariate, plus beta 0. The three covariate columns give 4 parameters.

66
00:12:19,970 --> 00:12:27,031
Step 4: write the model for one row with its numbers. The betas and the error of that row are unknown.

67
00:12:27,481 --> 00:12:47,428
Two questions of type B on the data of slide 23. Question 1: write the simple model with head size for row 3, and name the unknown values. Question 2: in the model with three covariates, what is x 5 2, and how many betas are there? Pause the video and try it.

68
00:12:47,878 --> 00:12:52,827
Step 1 of card B: the outcome y is brain weight, the green column.

69
00:12:53,277 --> 00:13:12,733
Step 4: row 3 has head size 4261 and brain weight 1335. So 1335 equals beta 0, plus beta 1 times 4261, plus epsilon 3.

70
00:13:13,183 --> 00:13:25,813
The unknown values are beta 0, beta 1 and epsilon 3. The numbers 1335 and 4261 are data.

71
00:13:26,263 --> 00:13:35,564
Question 2. Step 2: x 5 2 is covariate 2 of row 5. Covariate 2 is gender, so x 5 2 is M.

72
00:13:36,014 --> 00:13:41,305
Step 3: the three covariate columns plus beta 0 give 4 parameters.

73
00:13:41,755 --> 00:13:55,728
Now the normal distribution. Z follows a normal distribution with parameters mu and sigma squared. The blue curve is the standard normal: mu equals 0, and sigma squared equals 1.

74
00:13:56,178 --> 00:14:14,162
The density is 1 over the square root of 2 pi sigma squared, times e to the power minus z minus mu, squared, over 2 sigma squared. The exponent is 0 at z equals mu. So the peak is at mu, and the curve is symmetric about mu.

75
00:14:14,612 --> 00:14:21,545
A change in mu moves the curve. The green curve has mu equals 2. Its shape stays the same.

76
00:14:21,995 --> 00:14:33,835
A change in sigma squared changes the width. The orange curve has sigma squared equals 4. It is wider and lower, because the total area must stay equal to 1.

77
00:14:34,285 --> 00:14:47,555
The moments: the expected value of Z is mu. The variance of Z is sigma squared. The standard deviation sigma is the distance from the peak to the point where the curve changes its bend.

78
00:14:48,005 --> 00:14:59,162
Question type C: evaluate the normal density at a point. The likelihood in Lecture 2 multiplies such values. Method card C has four steps.

79
00:14:59,612 --> 00:15:04,199
Step 1: read the mean mu, the variance sigma squared, and the point z.

80
00:15:04,649 --> 00:15:13,395
Step 2: the exponent is minus z minus mu, squared, over 2 sigma squared. The orange arrow shows z minus mu.

81
00:15:13,845 --> 00:15:19,968
Step 3: take e to the power of the exponent. A point far from mu gives a small value.

82
00:15:20,418 --> 00:15:28,887
Step 4: divide by the square root of 2 pi sigma squared. The result is f of z, the height of the curve at z.

83
00:15:29,337 --> 00:15:36,036
A type C question. Pause the video and try it. Calculate f of 0 for the standard normal.

84
00:15:36,486 --> 00:15:45,083
Step 1 of card C: mu is 0, sigma squared is 1, and the point z is 0. The point is marked on the axis.

85
00:15:45,533 --> 00:15:53,512
Step 2: the exponent is minus 0 minus 0, squared, over 2. That is 0, because the point is at mu.

86
00:15:53,962 --> 00:15:56,586
Step 3: e to the power 0 is 1.

87
00:15:57,036 --> 00:16:05,740
Step 4: divide by the square root of 2 pi times 1. The square root of 2 pi is 2 point 5 0 6 6.

88
00:16:06,190 --> 00:16:13,785
So f of 0 is 0 point 3 9 8 9. It is the highest point of the standard normal curve.

89
00:16:14,235 --> 00:16:41,349
A type C question with course numbers. Pause the video and try it. Tutorial 1 gives the fitted line 68 point 4 5 minus 0 point 4 1 0 4 x, and sigma hat squared 20 point 8 2. With these estimates, the cost at 88 hours is normal. Evaluate its density at the observed cost 40. The likelihood of Lecture 2 multiplies such values.

90
00:16:41,799 --> 00:16:58,695
Step 1 of card C: mu is 68 point 4 5 minus 0 point 4 1 0 4 times 88, which is 32 point 3 3 5. Sigma squared is 20 point 8 2. The point is y equals 40.

91
00:16:59,145 --> 00:17:13,460
Step 2: the exponent. 40 minus 32 point 3 3 5 is 7 point 6 6 5. Square it, and divide by 2 times 20 point 8 2, which is 41 point 6 4.

92
00:17:13,910 --> 00:17:28,587
7 point 6 6 5 squared is 58 point 7 5. Divided by 41 point 6 4, this is 1 point 4 1 1. So the exponent is minus 1 point 4 1 1.

93
00:17:29,037 --> 00:17:35,757
Step 3: e to the power minus 1 point 4 1 1 is 0 point 2 4 3 9.

94
00:17:36,207 --> 00:17:50,394
Step 4: divide by the square root of 2 pi times 20 point 8 2, which is 11 point 4 4. So f of 40 is 0 point 0 2 1 3: the height of the green line.

95
00:17:50,844 --> 00:18:03,943
Slide 25 also gives a key rule. Start with n independent normal variables, Z 1 to Z n. Variable Z i has mean mu i and variance sigma i squared.

96
00:18:04,393 --> 00:18:09,427
Multiply each Z i by a constant a i, and add a constant b i.

97
00:18:09,877 --> 00:18:19,456
Add all the terms. Call the sum U. The rule says: U is also normal. A linear combination of independent normals is normal.

98
00:18:19,906 --> 00:18:23,703
The mean of U is the sum of a i mu i plus b i.

99
00:18:24,153 --> 00:18:38,895
The variance of U is the sum of a i squared, times sigma i squared. The constants b i are not in the variance: a shift does not change the spread. The rule holds with a different variance for each i.

100
00:18:39,345 --> 00:18:55,985
Why do the mean and the variance of U have this form? Start with one variable. By linearity of expectation, the mean of a Z plus b is a mu plus b. In the picture, a is 2 and b is 5: the mean moves from 0 to 5.

101
00:18:56,435 --> 00:19:08,787
Multiply by a, and the variance is multiplied by a squared. The orange curve, 2 Z, is twice as wide, with variance 4. The shift b does not change the width.

102
00:19:09,237 --> 00:19:16,511
For U, use line 1 for each term, and add. The mean of U is the sum of a i mu i plus b i.

103
00:19:16,961 --> 00:19:27,308
The Z i are independent. So the variance of the sum is the sum of the variances. The bar shows the variances of the terms side by side.

104
00:19:27,758 --> 00:19:33,198
Use line 2 for each term. Each term gives a i squared sigma i squared.

105
00:19:33,648 --> 00:19:45,445
Slide 25 states that U is normal. So U is normal with this mean and this variance. Question type D uses the two formulas in steps 3 and 4.

106
00:19:45,895 --> 00:20:01,106
Question type D: find the distribution of a linear combination of independent normals. Lecture 2 uses it for y i, and Lecture 3 uses it for beta 1 hat. Method card D has five steps.

107
00:20:01,556 --> 00:20:07,657
Step 1: check the conditions. Each Z i is normal, and the Z i are independent.

108
00:20:08,107 --> 00:20:14,337
Step 2: read the constants a i and b i, and the means and variances of the Z i.

109
00:20:14,787 --> 00:20:18,733
Step 3: the mean is the sum of a i mu i plus b i.

110
00:20:19,183 --> 00:20:25,797
Step 4: the variance is the sum of a i squared sigma i squared. The b i are not used.

111
00:20:26,247 --> 00:20:30,513
Step 5: write U as normal, with this mean and this variance.

112
00:20:30,963 --> 00:20:52,681
A type D question. Pause the video and try it. Z 1, Z 2 and Z 3 are independent normals, each with variance 1. Their means are 1, 2 and 300. U is 2 Z 1 plus 1, plus minus Z 2, plus 0 point 5 Z 3 minus 3. Find the distribution of U.

113
00:20:53,131 --> 00:20:59,637
Step 1 of card D: each Z i is normal, and they are independent. So the rule applies.

114
00:21:00,087 --> 00:21:13,506
Step 2: read the constants from U. a is 2, minus 1 and 0 point 5. b is 1, 0 and minus 3. The means are 1, 2 and 300, and each variance is 1.

115
00:21:13,956 --> 00:21:33,241
Step 3: the mean terms. Term 1 is 2 times 1 plus 1, which is 3. Term 2 is minus 1 times 2, which is minus 2. Term 3 is 0 point 5 times 300 minus 3, which is 147. The sum is 148.

116
00:21:33,691 --> 00:21:51,163
Step 4: the variance terms. 2 squared times 1 is 4. Minus 1, squared, times 1 is 1. 0 point 5 squared times 1 is 0 point 2 5. The sum is 5 point 2 5. The b values are not used.

117
00:21:51,613 --> 00:21:58,056
Step 5: U is normal with mean 148 and variance 5 point 2 5.

118
00:21:58,506 --> 00:22:17,791
A practice question of type D. Z 1 is normal with mean 1 and variance 4. Z 2 is normal with mean 3 and variance 4. They are independent. Find the distribution of U equals 3 Z 1 minus 2 Z 2 plus 5. Pause the video and try it.

119
00:22:18,241 --> 00:22:23,937
Step 1 of card D: both are normal, and they are independent. The rule applies.

120
00:22:24,387 --> 00:22:34,457
Step 2: a 1 is 3 and b 1 is 5. a 2 is minus 2 and b 2 is 0. The means are 1 and 3, and both variances are 4.

121
00:22:34,907 --> 00:22:44,443
Step 3: the mean terms are 3 times 1 plus 5, which is 8, and minus 2 times 3, which is minus 6. The mean is 2.

122
00:22:44,893 --> 00:23:00,274
Step 4: the variance terms are 3 squared times 4, which is 36, and minus 2, squared, times 4, which is 16. Each square is positive, so the variance is 36 plus 16, which is 52.

123
00:23:00,724 --> 00:23:11,583
Step 5: U is normal with mean 2 and variance 52. The standard deviation is the square root of 52, about 7 point 2 1.

124
00:23:12,033 --> 00:23:32,107
A type D question from Lecture 2, slide 13. Pause the video and try it. The model says y i equals beta 0 plus beta 1 x i plus epsilon i, with independent normal errors. The x i are fixed numbers. Find the distribution of y i.

125
00:23:32,557 --> 00:23:36,739
Step 1 of card D: the errors are normal and independent.

126
00:23:37,189 --> 00:23:57,029
Step 2: y i is the error times 1, plus the constant beta 0 plus beta 1 x i. So a is 1, and b is beta 0 plus beta 1 x i. The error has mean 0 and variance sigma squared. The dashed line is beta 0 plus beta 1 x.

127
00:23:57,479 --> 00:24:06,268
Step 3: the mean is 1 times 0, plus beta 0 plus beta 1 x i. So the mean of y i is on the line.

128
00:24:06,718 --> 00:24:16,190
Step 4: the variance is 1 squared times sigma squared, which is sigma squared. The orange curves show the same spread at each x.

129
00:24:16,640 --> 00:24:30,827
Step 5: y i is normal with mean beta 0 plus beta 1 x i and variance sigma squared, independent over i. Slide 13 of Lecture 2 writes the model in this form.

130
00:24:31,277 --> 00:24:57,730
The last type D question previews Lecture 3. Pause the video and try it. Beta 1 hat is the sum of w i times y i. The weight w i is x i minus x bar, over S x x. It is a fixed number. For the Tutorial 1 hours, x bar is 101 and S x x is 1294. Find the distribution of beta 1 hat.

131
00:24:58,180 --> 00:25:07,780
Step 1 of card D: the y i are normal and independent, from the last example. Beta 1 hat is a linear combination of them.

132
00:25:08,230 --> 00:25:23,846
Step 2: a i is w i, and b i is 0. The mean of y i is beta 0 plus beta 1 x i, and the variance is sigma squared. The bars show the seven weights for the Tutorial 1 hours.

133
00:25:24,296 --> 00:25:39,656
Step 3: the mean is the sum of w i times beta 0 plus beta 1 x i. Split it into beta 0 times the sum of w i, plus beta 1 times the sum of w i x i.

134
00:25:40,106 --> 00:25:49,749
The deviations x i minus 101 add to 0. So the sum of the weights is 0, and the beta 0 term drops out.

135
00:25:50,199 --> 00:26:07,457
The sum of w i x i is the sum of x i minus x bar, times x i, over S x x. The top is 1294, the same as S x x. So the sum is 1, and the mean is beta 1.

136
00:26:07,907 --> 00:26:34,382
Step 4: the variance is sigma squared times the sum of w i squared. The squared deviations add to 1294, so the sum of w i squared is 1294 over 1294 squared, which is 1 over 1294. The variance is sigma squared over 1294.

137
00:26:34,832 --> 00:26:52,795
Step 5: beta 1 hat is normal, with mean beta 1 and variance sigma squared over 1294. In general, the variance is sigma squared over S x x. Lecture 3 uses this for confidence intervals and tests.

138
00:26:53,245 --> 00:27:04,807
To recap. Card A: when x increases by a, the mean of y changes by beta 1 times a. With the estimate, use beta 1 hat, and give the units of y.

139
00:27:05,257 --> 00:27:16,351
Card B reads a model. The outcome is y. x i j is covariate j of row i. There is one beta for each covariate, plus beta 0.

140
00:27:16,801 --> 00:27:29,707
Card C evaluates the normal density: read mu, sigma squared and z, compute the exponent, take e to its power, and divide by the square root of 2 pi sigma squared.

141
00:27:30,157 --> 00:27:49,571
Card D: a linear combination of independent normals is normal. Its mean is the sum of a i mu i plus b i. Its variance is the sum of a i squared sigma i squared. Video 2 b builds the chi-square, t, and F distributions from standard normals.
